Webhook Delivery Flow

  1. Event Publishing

  User/Producer → POST /api/v1/events/publish → Event created in DB
  - Event is published with a schema ID and payload
  - System finds all active subscriptions for that schema
  - Creates delivery queue entries for each subscription

  2. Delivery Queue

  subscriptions table → delivery_queue table
  - Each subscription that matches the event schema gets a delivery entry
  - Status starts as queued
  - Includes: event data, subscriber webhook URL, retry config

  3. Webhook Worker Processing

  webhook_delivery_worker.py (polls every 2 seconds)
  The worker running in the background:
  - Polls delivery_queue for entries ready to send (status = queued or retries due)
  - Batches up to 10 deliveries per poll
  - Sends HTTP POST to subscriber's webhook URL
  - Includes HMAC signature in X-Webhook-Signature header

  4. Delivery Attempt

  POST https://subscriber-webhook-url.com/webhook
  Headers:
    X-Webhook-Signature: sha256=<hmac_hash>
    Content-Type: application/json
  Body:
    {event payload}

  5. Retry Logic (if delivery fails)

  Backoff Strategies:
  - Exponential (default): 1s → 2s → 4s → 8s → 16s → 32s...
  - Linear: 5s → 10s → 15s → 20s → 25s...
  - Fixed: 30s delay for all retries

  Max Retries: Configured per subscription (default: 3)

  Status Flow:
  queued → processing → failed → retrying → ...
                       ↓
                     completed (success)
                       ↓
                     failed (max retries) → DLQ

  6. Delivery Logs

  Every attempt is logged in delivery_logs table:
  - Request/response details
  - Latency
  - Status codes
  - Error messages
  - Retry attempt number

  7. Dead Letter Queue (DLQ)

  When max retries exceeded:
  - Delivery moved to delivery_dlq table
  - Can be manually retried later
  - Useful for debugging persistent failures

  8. Statistics Tracking

  Updates on subscriptions and event_messages tables:
  - total_deliveries
  - successful_deliveries
  - failed_deliveries
  - Success rate calculations

  Current System Status

  You can check the worker status:
  tail -f /tmp/webhook_worker.log

  Or check the queue:
  docker exec webhook-postgres psql -U webhook_user -d webhook_db -c "SELECT status, COUNT(*) FROM
  delivery_queue GROUP BY status;"




  Current Design: Long-running Worker Process

    Pros:
    - ✅ Simple, straightforward implementation
    - ✅ Low latency - polls every 2s, quick delivery
    - ✅ Good for development/testing
    - ✅ Predictable costs (just server resources)
    - ✅ Easy to debug with logs
    - ✅ No cold starts

    Cons:
    - ❌ Must run continuously (uses resources even when idle)
    - ❌ Single point of failure
    - ❌ Manual scaling needed for high volume
    - ❌ Requires process management (systemd, PM2, etc.)
    - ❌ Server needs to stay up 24/7

    Lambda-based Worker Design

    Pros:
    - ✅ Auto-scaling - handles traffic spikes automatically
    - ✅ Event-driven - only runs when needed
    - ✅ Cost-efficient at low volumes (pay per execution)
    - ✅ No server management - fully serverless
    - ✅ Built-in retry/DLQ - AWS handles failures
    - ✅ High availability - AWS manages redundancy

    Cons:
    - ❌ Cold starts (100-1000ms delay for first invocation)
    - ❌ 15-minute max timeout (might need to batch carefully)
    - ❌ More complex architecture (triggers, queues, IAM roles)
    - ❌ Cost at high volume can be higher than dedicated servers
    - ❌ Vendor lock-in to AWS
    - ❌ Harder to debug (distributed logs, no local testing)

    Recommended Hybrid Architecture

    For a production webhook system, I'd suggest both approaches working together:

    Architecture 1: SQS + Lambda (Recommended for AWS)

    Event Published → SQS Queue → Lambda Function → HTTP Webhook
                          ↓
                      DLQ for failures

    How it works:
    1. When event published → Create SQS message per subscription
    2. SQS triggers Lambda function (batch of 10)
    3. Lambda sends webhooks in parallel
    4. Failed deliveries → SQS visibility timeout (auto-retry)
    5. Max retries exceeded → Move to DLQ

    Benefits:
    - Serverless, scales to 1000s of concurrent executions
    - SQS handles retry logic built-in
    - Lambda timeout = 15 min (enough for slow webhooks)
    - Dead Letter Queue for failed deliveries

    Architecture 2: EventBridge + Step Functions (Advanced)

    Event → EventBridge → Step Function → Lambda (with retries)
    - More sophisticated orchestration
    - Built-in retry with exponential backoff
    - Better observability
    - Higher cost

    Architecture 3: Keep Current + Add Lambda for Bursts

    Normal load → Python worker (polls DB)
    High load/bursts → Also trigger Lambda via SNS/SQS
    - Hybrid: Cost-efficient baseline + burst capacity
    - Best of both worlds

    My Recommendation

    For your current stage (development/MVP):
    Keep the Python worker - it's simple and works well.

    For production (scale > 1000 webhooks/hour):
    Migrate to SQS + Lambda because:
    1. Auto-scaling handles traffic spikes (Black Friday, viral events)
    2. Cost-effective - only pay when webhooks are sent
    3. Reliable - AWS manages retries, failures, DLQ
    4. Less maintenance - no servers to manage

    Migration path:
    1. Current: Python worker ✓ (you are here)
    2. Add SQS queue to delivery_queue writes
    3. Add Lambda function that processes SQS messages
    4. Run both in parallel initially
    5. Turn off Python worker when Lambda proven stable

